# NOTE: the running harness actually loads openfga-combined.fga (this
# file's content plus openfga-example.fga's, as one model version — see
# that file's own doc comment for why). This file stays as the
# standalone, readable reference translation of just the depth-chain type.
#
# The depth-scaling check-latency workload's OpenFGA model — deliberately
# a SEPARATE, minimal model from openfga-example.fga, uploaded as its own
# authorization-model version in its own store. It exists to answer one
# question only: how does raw check latency grow with permission-chain
# depth, holding everything else fixed?
#
# A plain tuple-to-userset chain (`parent: [bench_node]`), NOT a
# nested-group-membership chain (`member: [bench_node#member]`) —
# `src/workload.ts`'s own `depthChainWorkload` doc comment covers why:
# OpenFGA's schema validator rejects a tupleset relation (the left side
# of `X from Y`) from carrying any userset-typed subject option at all,
# confirmed live against this harness's own OpenFGA instance. This shape
# instead mirrors `folder.parent->edit`/`document.parent->edit` from
# openfga-example.fga — already proven to work identically across all
# three engines — at a controlled, varying depth.
#
# One deliberate divergence from scripts/benchmark-check-depth.ts, spelled
# out here and in tools/rebac-benchmark/README.md: that script republishes
# a freshly-named namespace on every run (schema publish/versioning is a
# real, uniquely-authz feature — see docs/DECISIONS.md D-149). OpenFGA and
# SpiceDB models are not meant to be recreated per run at that frequency,
# so this benchmark instead defines `bench_node` ONCE per engine and
# isolates concurrent/repeat runs with unique object ids
# (`node_<runId>_<i>`) instead of a unique type.
model
  schema 1.1

type user

type bench_node
  relations
    define viewer: [user]
    define parent: [bench_node]
    # bench_node.view = viewer | parent->view (this repo's DSL spelling:
    # folder.view = viewer | edit | parent->view, same tuple-to-userset shape)
    define view: viewer or view from parent
